WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.820
For many people, when they think of Misery,
they think of the infamous “Hobbling Scene”.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:12.020
A scene where author Paul Sheldon, taken prisoner
by his self-proclaimed number one fan, is

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.710
hobbled to prevent any attempts at escaping.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:17.890
But the scene we saw in the movie is just
tame compared to the torture Paul endures

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:18.890
in the novel.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:22.040
Stick around to the end of this video to hear
why his journey was so painful.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.620
One of Stephen King’s strengths as a writer
is writing what he knows.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:33.660
As a result we’ve seen him create a lot
of characters who are authors, like Jack Torrance,

00:00:33.679 --> 00:00:35.390
Bill Denbrough and many others.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:39.800
These writers sometimes serve as a link between
his fictional stories and his own experiences.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.539
Of course, the fictional characters are heavily
dramatized, but there are still parallels

00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:44.539
we can draw.

00:00:44.539 --> 00:00:47.920
Welcome to Horror History, today we’re looking
at the renowned author of the best-selling

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.640
Misery books: Paul Sheldon.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.740
To fully appreciate Paul’s struggle, I’m
gonna take it all the way back to his birth.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.219
The son of a vacuum cleaner salesman, Paul
Sheldon was born 1945.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.780
He was raised in suburban Boston, so his family
often took him to Revere Beach when he was

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:07.460
young.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.980
He always insisted they sit by a piling in
the water that looked like the fang of a monster

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:14.240
so that he could keep an eye on it and the
tide rolled in and out.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.299
On one occasion, he cut his foot on a bottle
in the sand, and he cried, causing his father

00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:21.229
to tell him it was just a little cut, and
to stop acting like someone had cut his god****

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:21.780
foot off.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.220
If only he knew.

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:27.359
The first movie he saw as a kid was Bambi, and just as Bambi
would have to learn to live in the forest

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:31.009
without the care of his parents, Paul would
one day become very dependent on Annie for

00:01:31.009 --> 00:01:32.009
his own survival.

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:36.210
During one childhood trip to the Boston Zoo,
his mother took him to see a bird from Africa,

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:40.179
and young Paul cried because he knew the bird
would die in a cage far from home, and he

00:01:40.179 --> 00:01:43.570
thinks about this again when he finds himself
in a far away prison.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:47.320
For a couple summers his mom sent him to day
camp at Malden Community Center where he first

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:48.830
played a game called “Can You?”

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:52.759
The camp counselor would start a story where
a character is in some kind of predicament,

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:56.070
then pick a camper at random, and they would
have 10 seconds to continue it.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.740
The campers would then vote if it was plausible.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.820
If you mess up you have to leave the circle.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.370
Paul was quite good at this game, and it’s
probably a big reason he was able to make

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:07.210
it as an author of fiction; it was basically
a never ending game of “Can You.”

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.780
When Paul was 12 years old, during summer
vacation, his dad was working and his mom

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.810
went to spend the day in Boston with Mrs.
Kaspbrak from across the street.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.870
This is probably not the same Mrs. Kasprak
as Sonia Kaspbrak, the mother of Eddie from

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.530
IT, because Paul would have been twelve in
the year 1957, and as far as we know, Sonia

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.770
and Eddie were living in Derry, Maine at that
time.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.420
So it may be that Eddie had other relatives
near Boston.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.790
Anyway, when Paul’s parents are gone, he
smokes one his Mom’s cigarettes, and the

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:34.440
room filled up with smoke just as she came back,
having forgotten his purse.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.500
This could be seen as juvenile curiosity,
but may have been a warning of the addiction

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.920
problems Paul would deal with later in life.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.470
He was a heavy smoker for years in his adulthood.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.170
That fall he finished reading William Golding’s
Lord of the Flies and found himself rushing

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:49.190
to the bathroom to throw up moments later.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:53.440
On his 14th birthday, his father gave him
a condom, telling him to put it in his wallet,

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.620
apparently because he didn’t want to see
his son in the army by age 16.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.590
Paul cites this as one of the few instances
where his Dad ever paid him much attention.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:04.120
In high school, Paul played baseball, but
wasn’t much good at it due to his inability

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.120
to hit a curveball.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.470
He grew up to be a tall man with sandy colored
hair.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:11.400
He didn’t go far for college, and one night
at 3AM, he received a call from his mother,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.420
who told him to come home because his father
had had a stroke.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:20.000
He drove his old Ford Wagon 70mph, but was
too late, and lost one of his parents at a

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.070
very young age.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.420
As a side note, the Ford Station Wagon was
the same car that Stephen King drove when

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.700
he was college-aged, as explained in a 2002
article he wrote in The New Yorker.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.930
Paul’s first wife was named Eileen, and
they went to Maui for their honeymoon where

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.120
they experienced a luau, where a pig was roasted
over an open fire.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.681
It’s unclear if this was before or after
his wedding, but in his prime, Paul was a

00:03:38.681 --> 00:03:39.740
best-selling author.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:43.430
He was featured in People magazine after writing
his first best-seller… then he ended up

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:45.880
in Us magazine after his first divorce.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:50.070
He ended up getting a second wife… and a
second divorce.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:54.650
Such is the life of a famous writer, although
King himself will celebrate his 50th anniversary

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.260
in 2021, so maybe that’s just a trope.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:01.350
Paul owns homes in both New York and Los Angeles,
which he was able to afford based on the success

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:05.260
of his most popular book series, the Misery
novels, a romance series set in the Victorian

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:09.800
era and follows the crazy adventures of Misery
Chastain and her suitors, Ian and Geoffrey.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.930
Though Misery proved to be his cash cow, Paul
grew to hate Misery since the third book,

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.190
because he felt the series distracted critics
from what he considered to be his “real

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.190
work”.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:21.680
He also got tons of fanmail, the craziest
of which he considered to be a 1-page letter

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.810
from a Mrs. Roman D (Virginia) Sandpiper who
included seven polaroid photos of original

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:30.260
furnishings in her recreation of Misery’s
parlor slash study.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:34.440
He made the mistake of replying, and was hit
with another letter, this time 10 pages with

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.440
40 polaroids.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.900
Paul didn’t answer this one, but that didn’t
stop her from following up with at least 5

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:39.900
more letters.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:43.930
The marriage thing may have been made up,
but I can definitely see Stephen King… projecting

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:46.090
something by including these details.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:49.960
Personally, as far as fan art goes, I still
love receiving them, and am flattered every

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:50.460
time.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.060
I wonder if King has ever seen an episode
of Horror History...

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.420
Paul is open with his friends and acquaintances
about the fact that he doesn’t take the

00:04:56.420 --> 00:05:01.030
Misery books seriously, and on April Fools
Day 1983, he sent a mock booklet to some of

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:04.880
them, which was titled Misery’s Hobby, which
presumably made fun of his own character.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.520
As he got older, the addictive side of him
took a stronger hold, and he was often out

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.830
bar hopping, smoking a lot of cigarettes and
sleeping with multiple women.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:15.430
This slowed down his productivity behind the
typewriter, and while I was only speculating

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:19.560
about the fanmail being inspired from King’s
real experiences, this is something that definitely

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:20.940
stemmed from his real life.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.320
At least the drug addiction part of it.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.170
I would go so far as to say that this is what
Misery is about more than anything else.

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:27.170
That is…

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:30.250
Misery the Stephen King novel, not Misery
the Paul Sheldon novel.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:34.220
In his 2000 memoir, On Writing, King admits
that he dealt with serious drug and alcohol

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.130
problems in the 80s, and barely remembers
writing Cujo or The Tommyknockers, and

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.250
Misery came out the same year as the latter.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:43.630
King feels that his writing improved after
he got his act together, and this is the most

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:47.100
important takeaway for Paul’s character,
as the habits prevent him from reaching his

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:48.150
full potential.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.820
While we know that Misery’s Child was his
ninth novel overall, we don’t know exactly

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:54.720
how many Misery books there are, but we do
know about several of them.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.400
I’m assuming the first book is just titled
Misery, because Paul at one point refers to

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.620
it in his head as “the first Misery book”
rather than giving it a proper title.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:04.680
Other titles mentioned include Misery’s
Quest and Misery’s Love.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.210
The movie details way more Misery books that
we see in Annie’s collection, but for this

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.290
video we’re sticking to the canon of the
book.

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:14.610
Paul killed off the titular character at the
end of Misery’s Child, and celebrated, because

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.820
this meant he would never have to write another
Misery book again.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:19.720
Or so he thought.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:32.419
After severing himself from the Misery franchise,
Paul got an idea for a new book one day in

00:06:32.419 --> 00:06:36.460
New York City after walking past an attendant
at a parking lot trying to jimmy his way into

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:37.460
a car.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:41.060
The project would be called Fast Cars, and
feature a car thief named Tony Bonasaro

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.610
from East Harlem in New York.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:45.900
He started work on this one in 1985, when
he had turned 40 years old.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.640
He interviewed crash victims as part of the
research for the books finale, and as with

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:52.820
all of his books, he did not create a backup
copy of the manuscript.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:56.740
He would spend two years working on it, and
as part of his personal tradition for finishing

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:01.590
a book, once he got close to the end, he moved
his operation to The Boulderado Hotel in Colorado,

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:04.190
where he would enjoy a cigar and a bottle
of Champagne.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:08.450
He considered Fast Cars to be his best work,
and the day before it’s completion, he deposited

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:12.680
a $450 check at the Boulder Bank for a little
spending money, not knowing he’d have nothing

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:15.130
to spend the majority of it on for quite some
time.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:18.240
The book was finished in mid to late February
1987.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.830
A snowstorm was brewing in the area, but meteorologists
predicted it would turn and go South, so Paul

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:26.180
figured this was the perfect chance to beat
the weather and start back to New York while

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:27.180
he still could.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:31.040
However, he rushed out a little too quickly
after drinking the Champagne and ended up

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.080
regrettably driving under the influence.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.470
That’s one of the factors that led to his
car accident.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:39.160
It happened on Route 9, when he leaned forward
to get his cigarette, temporarily taking his

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:40.160
eyes off the road.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.800
The event that would follow would completely
change his life, and I find it to be a symbol

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:47.669
for King’s addiction problems, in that the
crash was effectively caused by two things:

00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:49.169
alcohol and cigarettes.

00:07:49.169 --> 00:07:53.300
The next thing he knew, his 1974 Camaro had
flipped over, sending him off the mountain

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:57.229
road and down a short slope, where his car
buried itself in the snow.

00:07:57.229 --> 00:08:00.580
Luckily, or perhaps unluckily for him, someone
heard his screams.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.240
Her name was Annie Wilkes.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.270
And she claimed to be his number one fan.

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:07.880
She may have saved his life with the CPR she
performed on him, and one of the last things

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:11.900
he remembered was her stinky breath as she
did so, which was also symbolic, because yes,

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:16.280
she was helping him stay alive, but her hospitality
always came with unwanted side effects.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:20.250
Paul passes out, and she brings him back to
her remote house, where he goes into a respiratory

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:22.190
depression for the next two weeks.

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:26.010
She feeds him through an IV, and he doesn’t
come out of the coma until early March, waking

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:28.340
up in severe pain to find his legs are shattered.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:31.690
He’s in constant pain, but at times it’s
more noticeable than others.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:35.999
She does give him a pain pill called Novril,
but it’s main ingredient is Codeine, a highly

00:08:35.999 --> 00:08:38.110
addictive opioid that Paul becomes addicted
to.

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:42.329
His body begins to need the drug despite the
bad side effects, just as he needs Annie,

00:08:42.329 --> 00:08:43.329
despite her bad side effects.

00:08:43.329 --> 00:08:47.819
I say that because Annie has a crazy case
of borderline personality disorder and Paul

00:08:47.819 --> 00:08:51.670
is just as much her prisoner as he is her
patient, just as a drug addict may become

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:53.850
a prisoner of the drugs he or she is taking.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:57.569
Novril is a fictional drug, you won’t find
it at your local Walgreens or… that back

00:08:57.569 --> 00:08:58.819
alley that you go to.

00:08:58.819 --> 00:09:01.690
There is a different thing called Noveryl,
but I think it’s more recent and it’s

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:02.690
not a pain medication.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:06.420
But I think the name Novril was named as such
as a reference to Paul’s other addiction

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:07.529
Novels.

00:09:07.529 --> 00:09:11.100
When Paul starts to lose hope, writing becomes
the only thing that keeps him going and helps

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.490
him cope with his pain.

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.661
Annie had also rescued some of Paul’s things
from the car wreck, and asks if she can read

00:09:15.661 --> 00:09:17.779
Fast Cars, a permission that he grants.

00:09:17.779 --> 00:09:22.139
The next day, she criticizes it after having
read 40 pages; in particular, the use of profanity,

00:09:22.139 --> 00:09:25.019
which was not present in the Misery novels,
bothers her.

00:09:25.019 --> 00:09:28.470
She gets really worked up about it and throws
Paul’s soup bowl against the wall where

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:31.640
it shatters, before making the recommendation
that he stick to the Misery books.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:35.250
Paul is in a lot of pain and asks for his
Novril, but she tortures him by making him

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:39.010
wait all afternoon because she’s busy cleaning
up the mess made by the soup bowl, a mess

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:40.769
that she blames him for making.

00:09:40.769 --> 00:09:44.100
When she finally gives him the pill, she forces
him to wash it down with rinse water from

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:45.300
the cleaning bucket.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:48.910
Addicts are often characterized with a common
phrase: “I gotta have my fix.”

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:51.720
Annie’s insistence that he do something
disgusting like that if he wants the pain

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:55.529
medication establishes the control that Annie
and the Novril have over him.

00:09:55.529 --> 00:09:57.320
When he wakes up the next morning Annie is…

00:09:59.329 --> 00:10:03.480
NOT OK, because she just read the ending of
Misery’s Child, where Misery dies, and she’s

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.040
a little bit pissed at Paul for killing off
her favorite character.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:08.940
“I DON’T WANT HER SPIRIT!

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.880
I WANT HER! AND YOU MURDERED HER!”

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.959
Just a little… upset.

00:10:15.959 --> 00:10:19.120
Annie nearly bashes him with a water pitcher,
then flings it at the door.

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:22.449
Unable to control her anger, she decides it’s
“unwise to be around him” and leaves to

00:10:22.449 --> 00:10:24.649
go to what she refers to as her “laughing
place.”

00:10:24.649 --> 00:10:28.380
She would be gone for the next 51 hours, Paul
which Paul kept track of my making marks on

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:29.380
his arm.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:32.910
During this time he discovered his splinted
legs, which were in worse shape than he thought,

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:36.420
and in a desperate attempt to relieve his
bladder and thirst, he’s actually forced

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:39.649
to go all Joey Salads and funnel his urine
into a cup and drink it.

00:10:39.649 --> 00:10:43.199
This is again a metaphor for the extremes
that an addict is willing to go to.

00:10:43.199 --> 00:10:46.240
Whether he likes it or not, Annie's hospitality
is his drug.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:49.860
When she finally returns, he’s in great
pain after not having taken any medication

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:50.860
for over two days.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:55.000
She brings in a charcoal grill and asks Paul
to burn his manuscript for Fast Cars, the

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:58.920
result of his last two years of work, and
refuses to give him any food or Novril until

00:10:58.920 --> 00:10:59.920
he does so.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:03.290
After a long struggle, he cooks the book,
and this represents one of the later stages

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:05.679
of addiction, where it starts to affect loved
ones.

00:11:05.679 --> 00:11:09.179
Paul doesn’t have any family, so we see
this represented by him destroying the thing

00:11:09.179 --> 00:11:10.380
that he cares most about.

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:12.730
There’s even a moment where he asks Annie
to do it for him.

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:14.769
“You burn it, then!” he yelled at her.

00:11:14.769 --> 00:11:15.929
She turned and looked at him.

00:11:15.929 --> 00:11:19.360
“No,” she said, “I cannot do that, as
much as I would like to and spare you the

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:20.360
agony you feel.”

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:21.360
“Why not?”

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:25.120
“Because,” she said primly, “you must
do it of your own free will.”

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:28.290
After this, she puts him in a wheelchair,
cleans his bed, and gives him a sponge bath.

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:32.610
He’s able to estimate that they’re somewhere
between 15 and 45 miles away from the town

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:37.220
of Sidewinder based on her trips, and figures
his crashed car is probably in a similar radius,

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:40.249
but may be buried in snow so he holds out
for the Spring melt.

00:11:40.249 --> 00:11:44.410
The next morning she brings in an old Royal
typewriter that he refers to as an antique.

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:47.790
The “N” key is missing, which according
to this book, 3000 Facts About Horror Movies

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:51.850
by James Egan, is a reference to one of Stephen
King’s first typewriters which had the same

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:52.850
problem.

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:55.309
That’s fact one-thousand eight hundred fifty-five
if you’re curious.

00:11:55.309 --> 00:11:59.110
He asks he what he’ll write with this high-tech
piece of machinery already knowing what she’ll

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:02.579
probably say, a new novel, just for her, called
Misery’s Return.

00:12:02.579 --> 00:12:05.199
He asks if she’ll let him go when he’s
done, to which she responds:

00:12:05.199 --> 00:12:07.910
“You speak as though I were keeping you
prisoner, Paul.”

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:11.519
Which she is, but I like how that line fits
into the drug metaphor, where an addict can

00:12:11.519 --> 00:12:15.209
technically stop any time they want, but it’s
very difficult because their bodies becomes

00:12:15.209 --> 00:12:16.529
dependent on the drug.

00:12:16.529 --> 00:12:20.679
This novel was to be her payment for nursing
him, but his time would not be the only thing

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:22.320
that Paul has to give up.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:32.670
Paul explains that he’ll need Annie to fetch
her other Misery books as reference since

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:35.990
he doesn’t have his concordance, hoping
to win some points with her by sharing some

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:39.949
behind the scenes author stuff, but she surprisingly
doesn’t seem that interested.

00:12:39.949 --> 00:12:42.929
When she brings him dinner that night, he
asks that she turns the typewriter away from

00:12:42.929 --> 00:12:46.579
him as part of a superstition, but the real
reason was that he didn’t like the way it’s

00:12:46.579 --> 00:12:48.380
“teeth” were almost grinning at him.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.839
The following morning she shakes him awake
to serve him his breakfast, excited for him

00:12:51.839 --> 00:12:55.070
to begin the novel, and Paul comes to the
realization that he might actually have a

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:58.869
chance at survival if she wants to read the
end of Misery’s Return as much as he thinks

00:12:58.869 --> 00:12:59.869
she does.

00:12:59.869 --> 00:13:02.069
Before starting, he asks her for a different
type of paper.

00:13:02.069 --> 00:13:04.879
This is where the mind games between Paul
and Annie really begin.

00:13:04.879 --> 00:13:08.869
She thinks he’s trying to stall so he doesn’t
have to start writing, and he tries to establish

00:13:08.869 --> 00:13:11.470
the idea that there’s a lot about writing
she doesn’t know about.

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:15.210
For example, he asks her to bring him that
Webster Pot, and when she doesn’t know what

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:18.999
that is, he says it’s another name for the
jar of pens and pencils, named after someone

00:13:18.999 --> 00:13:19.999
named Daniel Webster.

00:13:19.999 --> 00:13:23.369
I find that hilarious, and I’ll definitely
be using that from now on.

00:13:23.369 --> 00:13:25.910
I’m trying to figure out what the YouTuber
equivalent of that would be.

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:27.689
Like how about this mini-tripod.

00:13:27.689 --> 00:13:29.660
We’ll call it a Broad Legs.

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:30.709
Named after Chris Broad.

00:13:30.709 --> 00:13:33.820
“And the end result is an awkwardly named
product.”

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:37.180
The idea behind confusing her was that it
would calm her anger, because it was harder

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:39.310
to be upset about something she did not understand.

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:42.059
And it does work… to some extent…

00:13:42.059 --> 00:13:45.839
He demonstrates that the paper she got causes
smudging, and although she agrees to buy him

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.439
a new pack, she does get quite upset.

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:50.780
“And what thanks do I get?

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:52.440
‘Oh, you brought the wrong paper Annie.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:54.240
I can’t write on this paper Annie!’

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:57.380
We’ll I’ll get you’re stupid paper but
you just better start showing me a little

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.940
more appreciation around here Mr. Man.”

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.580
In the book, she punches him in the knee and
threatens to hurt him again if he tries to trick

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:09.029
her again before storming out.

00:14:09.029 --> 00:14:12.221
However, when she marched forward to attack
him, a bobby pin fell out of her hair, and

00:14:12.221 --> 00:14:16.059
he used it to unlock the door, but it broke
just as he wiggled it open.

00:14:16.059 --> 00:14:19.339
When he gets through the door, he imagines
her standing there with a shotgun, which was

00:14:19.339 --> 00:14:22.629
the first sign of his paranoia and hallucinations
starting to kick in.

00:14:22.629 --> 00:14:26.309
He finds her Novril stash in the downstairs
bathroom and after taking them, he becomes

00:14:26.309 --> 00:14:28.149
self-aware of his own situation.

00:14:28.149 --> 00:14:30.889
“...he realized the medication wasn’t
just working on his legs.

00:14:30.889 --> 00:14:33.730
He had gotten his fix, if you wanted to be
perfectly vulgar about it.”

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:37.439
He steals five boxes, which he thinks will
go unnoticed, and plans to hide them under

00:14:37.439 --> 00:14:38.439
his mattress.

00:14:38.439 --> 00:14:41.850
Before going back, he looks for a phone in
the living room, but when he finds it, it

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:42.850
isn’t operational.

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:46.329
Hearing her car, he hurries back, accidentally
bumping the doorframe as he did so.

00:14:46.329 --> 00:14:49.829
When she gets back, he acts like he’s been
in a lot of pain to cover up the fact that

00:14:49.829 --> 00:14:53.149
he’s covered in sweat from the mad dash
back into the room, so she gives him another

00:14:53.149 --> 00:14:56.839
two pills, which he has to take to keep up
his side of the story, and shortly after taking

00:14:56.839 --> 00:14:58.829
them he falls unconscious for 14 hours.

00:14:58.829 --> 00:15:02.439
In terms of the drug addiction analogy, this
represents an overdose.

00:15:02.439 --> 00:15:05.839
The next day marks the third week of him being
at Annie’s and the beginning of another

00:15:05.839 --> 00:15:09.899
snowstorm, and two days after that, the first
chapter of Misery’s Return is complete.

00:15:09.899 --> 00:15:13.199
The goal of this chapter is of course, to
explain the return of the series’ title

00:15:13.199 --> 00:15:15.529
character, who had died at the end of Misery’s
Child.

00:15:15.529 --> 00:15:19.049
In the previous book, the character Geoffrey
rode for a doctor, but his horse tripped on

00:15:19.049 --> 00:15:21.869
a tollgate, so he never made it, and the doctor
never showed up.

00:15:21.869 --> 00:15:25.670
In this one, he writes the doctor showing
up to save Misery’s life, and Annie sees

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:28.569
this as a cheat, so it’s back to square
one for Sheldon.

00:15:28.569 --> 00:15:32.190
He feels drowsy at the typewriter, and decides
it would be a good idea to cut back on his

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:33.209
Novril addiction.

00:15:33.209 --> 00:15:36.439
He plans to hide one of the two pills each
time she gives them to him so he can start

00:15:36.439 --> 00:15:37.490
to ween off of it.

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:40.980
He decides he’ll start tomorrow, but then
pushes it back to after he has an acceptable

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:41.980
first chapter.

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:46.269
The mindset represents a common coping tactic
used by smokers and other drug addicts.

00:15:46.269 --> 00:15:49.739
They think they can slowly get themselves
off of it, but will continually put it off

00:15:49.739 --> 00:15:50.769
once the day comes.

00:15:50.769 --> 00:15:56.509
Having a small amount can lead to a relapse,
causing them to have to start all over -- just

00:15:56.509 --> 00:15:58.620
as Paul was about to start chapter 1 all over.

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:02.929
On March 25th, Paul is 6 chapters in, and
this time, Annie approves of the content and

00:16:02.929 --> 00:16:03.929
says it’s fair.

00:16:03.929 --> 00:16:07.319
He finds that this is the most gruesome of
the Misery books, and thinks it’s probably

00:16:07.319 --> 00:16:09.329
because he’s in a gruesome state of mind.

00:16:09.329 --> 00:16:12.809
In the afternoon he ponders what to do about
the black marks on the doorframe -- the spot

00:16:12.809 --> 00:16:14.999
where the wheelchair had hit when he snuck
out of the room.

00:16:14.999 --> 00:16:18.629
He thinks that if she hasn’t noticed them,
it’s likely she will soon.

00:16:18.629 --> 00:16:21.220
Just then she rushes into the room with handcuffs
and a dust cloth.

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:24.970
She cuffs him to the headboard and gags him
with the cloth so he can’t make any noise,

00:16:24.970 --> 00:16:27.579
and she orders him not to make a sound as a car
pulls up.

00:16:27.579 --> 00:16:31.620
Paul sees an older man get out and talk to
Annie just outside of his line of sight, and

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:33.309
hears her get very angry with him.

00:16:33.309 --> 00:16:36.540
Meanwhile, he tastes the cleaning chemicals
leftover on the rag and has to fight back

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:39.079
vomiting and subsequently choking himself.

00:16:39.079 --> 00:16:42.679
After the guy leaves she paces around Paul’s
room in a furious rage, mumbling something

00:16:42.679 --> 00:16:46.290
about a 10% tax increase, a fee and a lean
on her house.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:49.329
She finally takes out the rag, and Paul is
dry heaving as she does so.

00:16:49.329 --> 00:16:52.609
He examines the document that the guy had
given her and explains that she missed a tax

00:16:52.609 --> 00:16:55.999
payment on her house, and she still has a
chance to resolve it by the end of the day.

00:16:55.999 --> 00:16:59.879
Paul realizes he has an opportunity to get
in her good graces here, another example of

00:16:59.879 --> 00:17:02.639
him playing mind games to try to improve his
situation.

00:17:02.639 --> 00:17:06.560
His goal is to give her a false sense of security
around him by giving her the idea that he’s

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:07.560
on her side.

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:11.220
So he offers to give her the $400 left in
his wallet, the money from the check he cashed

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:12.299
the day before the crash.

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.510
Additionally, she’d have to go into town
to make the payment, and he planned to sneak

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:19.480
out again and get more materials to clean up the
marks on the doorframe while she was gone.

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:22.260
Before she leaves, he asks her to put him
in the wheelchair so he can write, and when

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:25.990
she’s gone he access a stash of bobby pins
he’d been hiding under the mattress.

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:29.230
He was able to pick the lock again and retrieve
some Kleenex, which he used to wipe away the

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:30.290
marks with water.

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:33.090
Not wanting to push his luck any further that
day, he went back to his writing.

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:36.820
This is a small detail, but I think it shows that
he’s starting to be broken in by Annie’s

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:37.820
overbearing attitude.

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:41.350
Earlier that month he was willing to risk
being caught out of the room for much longer,

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:43.540
but now he was afraid to explore much at all.

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:46.639
And the reason that he doesn’t want to get caught
is not just that he thought she’d be upset

00:17:46.639 --> 00:17:50.360
at him for sneaking past the locked door,
but also as part of the mental manipulation

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:52.049
that he’s been trying to use on her.

00:17:52.049 --> 00:17:55.309
He wants her to think he’s still broken
and helpless, so that when the time comes,

00:17:55.309 --> 00:17:57.290
he can really surprise her with his strength.

00:17:57.290 --> 00:18:00.429
He’s also been secretly working out his
arms when she’s out of the room by lifting

00:18:00.429 --> 00:18:02.169
the heavy typewriter up and down.

00:18:02.169 --> 00:18:05.659
Now that he’s cutting back on his intake
of the pills, his productivity also increases

00:18:05.659 --> 00:18:08.830
and he’s able to write an astounding twelve
pages per day.

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:14.370
By April 15th, he’s got 267 pages, but that
day, he would discover a horrible secret about

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:15.120
his captor.

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:26.480
When Annie Wilkes comes into Paul’s room
on April 15th, she looks like a complete mess.

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:30.929
She was covered in streaks of goo and had a
bloody lip as she threw the Novril pills across

00:18:30.929 --> 00:18:31.950
the room to Paul.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:33.610
He asks her if she is okay.

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:34.500
And she tells him.

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:37.480
No.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:39.160
She tells him no.

00:18:39.179 --> 00:18:41.919
She leaves the room, and he hears her slapping
herself outside.

00:18:41.919 --> 00:18:44.440
She did not come in to put him into his wheelchair
that morning.

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:48.730
The pills had relieved his first fix, but
he's still pining for his second fix: writing.

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:52.950
I think for Paul, writing symbolically represents
a substitute, something someone may use to

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:57.200
relieve their cravings when trying to quit
smoking, usually in the form of gum, candy,

00:18:57.200 --> 00:18:58.480
or even a fake cigarette.

00:18:58.480 --> 00:18:59.910
Or subscribing to CZsWorld on YouT--

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:03.740
Because he's become psychologically dependent
on the hormone released that he associates

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:07.340
with writing, he's unable to stop himself
from getting himself into the wheelchair,

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:10.769
even though he knows it's in his best interest
not to let Annie know that he has the strength

00:19:10.769 --> 00:19:11.600
to do this.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:13.160
Unfortunately for him…

00:19:13.169 --> 00:19:14.169
"It's a trap!"

00:19:14.169 --> 00:19:17.760
Annie was looking in at him through the keyhole,
and she screams at him if he's capable of

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:20.070
getting in the chair, he can fill in his own
Ns.

00:19:20.070 --> 00:19:22.159
The slip-up throws Paul off of his pace.

00:19:22.159 --> 00:19:24.730
He ends up not getting much done and going
back to bed.

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:28.910
He wakes up that afternoon, with Annie by
his bedside in a completely psychotic state.

00:19:28.910 --> 00:19:32.500
She has a cup full of pills and an injured
rat in a trap, which she kills with her bare

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:36.769
hand, and offers to get her gun and put Paul
and herself out of their misery as well!

00:19:36.769 --> 00:19:40.460
Coincidentally it would be Misery, the character,
that saves Paul's life.

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:43.929
He tells her he wants to finish the book,
and she agrees that it's only thing she has

00:19:43.929 --> 00:19:45.299
left to look forward to in life.

00:19:45.299 --> 00:19:47.910
She then licks up the rat's blood from her
fingers.

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:51.380
You'd think a nurse would be a little more
conscious about disease.

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:54.500
She has to go away to her laughing place for
a while, and doesn't know how long she'll

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:58.419
be gone, so two hours later Paul picks the
lock to the guestroom door to find a huge

00:19:58.419 --> 00:20:01.149
mess of dishes and the remains of several
unhealthy snacks.

00:20:01.149 --> 00:20:03.630
Her ceramic figures were also thrown about
the room.

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:07.980
He looks for a possible escape method, but
Annie has installed 3 locks on every door.

00:20:07.980 --> 00:20:11.740
He retrieved some food from the pantry, just
enough to sustain him while she was gone without

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:13.730
her hopefully noticing he'd taken any.

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:17.669
On the way back to his room he notices a scrapbook
with the words "memory lane".

00:20:17.669 --> 00:20:21.820
It contains newspapers documenting Annie's
entire life, most of them consisted of her most

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:25.830
likely killing her patients at the various
nursing jobs that she held over the years.

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:28.100
The final page is an article from Newsweek.

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:30.559
Author Paul Sheldon, 42, reported missing.

00:20:30.559 --> 00:20:33.559
Paul thinks to himself that it's almost like
reading his own obituary.

00:20:33.559 --> 00:20:35.690
That seems to be a concept that King kind
of likes.

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:39.470
I'm reminded of It, where Richie discovers
the flyer for the Richie Tozier All Dead Rock

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:40.020
Show.

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:44.300
"That's my shirt! That's my hair! That's my face!"

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:45.380
"Calm down, this isn't real."

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:46.860
"That's my name! That's my age!! That's the date!!"

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:50.820
But I think this moment, more than anything,
is supposed to represent a wake-up call.

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:54.570
I imagine when King was dealing with his own
addiction problems, there was something like

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:58.230
this, a moment that made him realize that
if he continued down the same path, he was

00:20:58.230 --> 00:20:59.380
not going to make it.

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:03.009
In the late eighties, King would take the
steps to kill his addictions, and on April

00:21:03.009 --> 00:21:06.830
16th Paul would brainstorm some ideas to kill
Annie, and ultimately decides on the simple

00:21:06.830 --> 00:21:10.419
tactic of stealing a knife from the kitchen
and hiding it until she gets back.

00:21:10.419 --> 00:21:14.890
But Paul was fast asleep the next morning
at 4AM when she returned and discovered what

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:15.890
he had done.

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:19.140
When he eventually starts to wake up, she
injects him with a needle that slowly numbs

00:21:19.140 --> 00:21:20.289
his entire body.

00:21:20.289 --> 00:21:24.169
She tells him there’s good news and bad
news, but the good news is just that Paul's

00:21:24.169 --> 00:21:28.100
car disappeared in the Spring run-off after
the snow melted… which is actually bad news

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:32.049
for Paul because he was counting on them eventually
finding his car in order to hopefully locate

00:21:32.049 --> 00:21:33.049
him.

00:21:33.049 --> 00:21:34.550
The bad news is much worse.

00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:37.929
Annie had not only discovered the broken chunk
of the bobby pin he used to sneak out the

00:21:37.929 --> 00:21:41.840
first time he left the room, but she’d also
set up threads all over the house that were

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.690
now broken, proving that he’d been rummaging
around.

00:21:44.690 --> 00:21:48.660
She explains the story of the Kimberly Diamond
Mine, where the British would injure the native

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:52.420
workers if they tried to steal, ensuring that
they could continue work, but they would not

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:53.420
be able to run.

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:55.720
“The operation was called hobbling.”

00:21:55.720 --> 00:21:59.740
And that’s exactly what Annie planned to
do to Paul, ensuring he could finish her book,

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:01.269
without fear of him running away.

00:22:01.269 --> 00:22:04.941
The mine that she mentioned is actually located
in Kimberly, South Africa, coincidentally

00:22:04.941 --> 00:22:08.940
fitting in with the metaphor of Paul being
a caged African bird far away from home, an

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:12.370
idea he had been channeling in his writing
by making the bulk of Misery’s Return take

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:13.490
place in Africa.

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:17.830
The hobbling scene in the movie is already
considered one of the most uncomfortable horror

00:22:17.830 --> 00:22:21.120
scenes, but what happens in the book takes
it at least two steps further.

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:25.090
She slathers his leg in Betadine, and pulls
out an axe, which she uses to cut through

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:26.920
his ankle on her second hack.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:28.940
"AHHHHHHHHHH!!!!"

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.040
"Almost done."

00:22:31.450 --> 00:22:36.120
Then she pulls out a blowtorch uses it to
cauterize the wound, before finally dumping

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:39.940
a bucket of water over the burnt, blackened
stump that had once been an ankle, in order

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:41.470
to put out the flame.

00:22:41.470 --> 00:22:45.350
Paul passed out as she left the room and was
in a semi-comatose state for a week.

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:48.590
He was barely breathing because of the heavy
doses of pain medication.

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:52.299
When he eventually got back to work, he was
only able to sit up in short spurts, so he

00:22:52.299 --> 00:22:53.659
didn’t get as much done.

00:22:53.659 --> 00:22:57.330
Some unspecified amount of time passes, and
it’s either May or June when Annie brings

00:22:57.330 --> 00:23:00.980
in Ice Cream Sundae and essentially tries
to bribe Paul into telling her the rest of

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:05.000
the story of Misery’s Return, which he of
course refuses, giving her a reason to keep

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:07.539
him alive -- at least long enough to finish
writing it.

00:23:07.539 --> 00:23:10.159
A foot would not be the only body part he
lost.

00:23:10.159 --> 00:23:13.660
Annie’s next acquisition would be even more
evil than the first.

00:23:13.660 --> 00:23:17.220
It started one day when he complained about
the antique typewriter, and it’s missing

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:19.659
N, so Annie decided to give him something
else to worry about.

00:23:19.659 --> 00:23:23.490
He heard her having a fit in the kitchen,
and she came back with the disinfectant, the

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.970
numbing agent and an electric knife.

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:29.429
Despite Paul's screams and pleas, his thumb
was gone by that evening.

00:23:29.429 --> 00:23:33.360
This must have been especially painful for
Paul, considering his entire livelihood depends

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:36.820
on typing, and writing the book was the one
thing that was able to distract him from the

00:23:36.820 --> 00:23:39.159
pain of his broken legs and severed foot.

00:23:39.159 --> 00:23:43.040
But it gets worse than that though, because
that evening she brought in a cake and started

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:44.600
singing him Happy Birthday.

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:46.750
There was just one issue with this...

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:49.789
"It's not my birthday!!!"

00:23:49.789 --> 00:23:52.710
His so-called number one fan probably knows
this.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:56.429
She told him: "If you promise to be good Paul
you can have a piece of birthday cake but

00:23:56.429 --> 00:23:59.009
you won’t have to eat any of the special
candle."

00:23:59.009 --> 00:24:03.230
On June 21st, another character falls out of
the typewriter, but this time he doesn't dare

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:04.230
complain.

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:07.639
The letter E falls out soon after, which sucks
because that's the most commonly used letter

00:24:07.639 --> 00:24:08.960
in the English language.

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:10.400
"E".

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:14.710
Over a month after losing his thumb Paul wakes
up to the sound of a car in the driveway,

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:16.129
and discovers that it's a police car.

00:24:16.129 --> 00:24:20.690
By all means, this should have been the end
of Paul's now 5 months of captivity, but we

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:24.879
end up seeing the psychological effects of
Annie's treatment of him here more than ever.

00:24:24.879 --> 00:24:28.669
He's been fantasizing about being rescued
for so long, that at first he thinks it's

00:24:28.669 --> 00:24:30.250
only in his imagination.

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:34.259
Then when he realizes that it's real, he finds
that he's been conditioned not to say anything.

00:24:34.259 --> 00:24:38.490
Because of the abusive punishments like leaving
him home alone with no food or medicine, chopping

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:42.970
off his foot and cutting off his thumb, he
is physically unable to bring himself to scream,

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:47.070
because some part of his brain is anticipating
Annie's punishments and holding him back.

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:50.520
After several moments of internal struggle,
he's able to finally belt out the first word

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:51.660
that comes to his head.

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:55.080
"Africa!!!"

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:59.919
The cop hears him, but seems uncertain where
the sound came from, so Paul grabs a ceramic

00:24:59.919 --> 00:25:02.559
ashtray from off the table and throws it through
the window.

00:25:02.559 --> 00:25:06.539
The cop crosschecks a photograph to confirm
that it's the missing author Paul Sheldon,

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:10.180
but unfortunately for Paul, the realisation
comes too late.

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:21.460
Dwayne Kushner was the cop that set out to
go door to door looking for leads about the

00:25:21.470 --> 00:25:23.510
location of Paul Sheldon that afternoon.

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:27.009
He had not expected to come across the man
he was looking for but upon the shock of the

00:25:27.009 --> 00:25:31.190
revelation that he’s done just that, Annie
stabs him multiple times with a cross before

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:32.721
he's able to draw his gun.

00:25:32.721 --> 00:25:36.860
This was a worst case scenario for Paul, who
is reaching a state of extreme desperation.

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:39.960
He considers going to the kitchen to get a
knife, with which he could threaten to slit

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:44.429
his wrists, effectively holding himself (and
the end of Misery's Return) hostage.

00:25:44.429 --> 00:25:48.279
But then he notices that the cop is still
alive and tries to warn him that she's coming

00:25:48.279 --> 00:25:49.279
back.

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:51.520
But Annie ends up running the guy over with
a ridable lawn mower.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:53.140
"BWAHHHH!!"

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:59.259
Even after watching his own body parts get
dismembered, this ends up being what pushes

00:25:59.259 --> 00:26:01.860
Paul over the edge and causes him to vomit.

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:05.460
After cleaning the mess, Annie tells him she's
not going to kill him, but she does move him

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:08.960
down to the basement cellar so she doesn't
have to worry about someone finding him while

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:10.909
she goes and hides the officer's car.

00:26:10.909 --> 00:26:14.289
She does warn him that she took the officer's
handgun, and if he tries anything when more

00:26:14.289 --> 00:26:18.860
police come looking for him, she won't hesitate
to shoot them and then kill Paul and herself.

00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:22.279
She makes up a whole scenario to tell the
cops where she supposedly sent the officer

00:26:22.279 --> 00:26:23.279
on his way.

00:26:23.279 --> 00:26:26.929
She explains her plan in great detail to Paul
so that he realizes that they're now in hot

00:26:26.929 --> 00:26:29.429
water and his time to finish the novel is
limited.

00:26:29.429 --> 00:26:32.920
She doesn't allow him to have a light so he
can continue working while she's gone, which

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:36.570
is distressing for him because he's afraid
of the rats that fill the cellar and because

00:26:36.570 --> 00:26:40.330
he knows he needs to keep his mind occupied
with writing in order to get his fix.

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:43.279
Annie's psychological intimidation impacts
him again that night.

00:26:43.279 --> 00:26:46.470
He can't stop thinking about the Dwayne’s
death and feels guilty for being partially

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:49.629
responsible, because Annie would have had
no reason to kill the guy if he hadn’t been

00:26:49.629 --> 00:26:50.629
tipped off.

00:26:50.629 --> 00:26:54.039
While she's gone, he discovers that the basement
is where Annie keeps the grill that she made

00:26:54.039 --> 00:26:55.650
him burn Fast Cars on.

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:58.990
He finds the can of lighter fluid and hides
it in the back of his underpants, hoping she

00:26:58.990 --> 00:27:02.289
doesn't notice when she carries him back up
the stairs the next morning.

00:27:02.289 --> 00:27:05.760
She doesn’t and when she’s out of the
room he hides it behind a loose piece of baseboard

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:06.760
on the wall.

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:10.700
I find this kind of ironic on a metafictional
level, and I’m pretty sure it’s intentional

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:14.190
because the reason that Annie rejects his
first attempt at chapter one is because it

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:16.289
contradicts the previous Misery novel.

00:27:16.289 --> 00:27:19.379
She goes on to explain that she doesn’t
care if an artist pulls a solution out of

00:27:19.379 --> 00:27:21.169
thin air, as long as it’s fair.

00:27:21.169 --> 00:27:25.260
Paul identifies this as a deus ex machina,
which he considers to be kind of an outdated

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:28.860
writing technique, but ultimately he has to
use it to make a first chapter that Annie

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:29.860
will accept.

00:27:29.860 --> 00:27:33.190
And in a way, King is kind of doing the same
thing here, because the loose piece of baseboard

00:27:33.190 --> 00:27:36.700
where he hides the lighter fluid is never
mentioned before, he just brings it up because

00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:39.919
it’s a convenient hiding place now that
the space underneath the mattress has been

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:40.919
compromised.

00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:44.649
The state police do show up the next day -- two
men named Wicks and McKnight, though Paul

00:27:44.649 --> 00:27:47.720
refers to them as David and Goliath because
he doesn’t know their names.

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:51.440
They ask some questions and poke around a
bit, but Paul doesn’t scream.

00:27:51.440 --> 00:27:55.000
Afterwards, Annie asks why, and he tells her
he just wants to finish the book in relative

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:55.740
peace.

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:59.220
His real motivation was that he thought there
was maybe a 20% chance she’s be able to

00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:03.580
catch them off guard and get the quick draw
on both of them, in which case she would probably

00:28:03.580 --> 00:28:04.629
kill Paul and herself.

00:28:04.629 --> 00:28:08.549
Also, after everything she had put him through,
he was starting to want more than to simply

00:28:08.549 --> 00:28:12.529
get her thrown in jail -- he had a plan to
hurt her in a way that only he could.

00:28:12.529 --> 00:28:15.950
The next five days were filled with visitors
coming to look at Annie’s house after hearing

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:18.769
that she was suspected in relation to Dwayne
Kushner’s disappearance.

00:28:18.769 --> 00:28:22.650
Curious people, news crews and teenagers showed
up at the house, and Paul made sure to stay

00:28:22.650 --> 00:28:26.370
out of sight of each of them, not wanting
to get any more innocent people killed.

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:30.179
On the fourth and fifth evenings, Paul stays
up writing until dawn, after which the day

00:28:30.179 --> 00:28:32.740
of the novel’s completion would finally
arrive.

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:36.870
Annie brings in a celebratory caviar, and
Paul asks her that he be allowed one cigarette

00:28:36.870 --> 00:28:41.140
along with the champagne that he usually indulges
in as part of his tradition for finishing

00:28:41.140 --> 00:28:41.840
a book.

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:45.880
She reluctantly allows it, and as Paul expects,
she doesn’t want to be around when he smokes

00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:46.380
it.

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:50.179
So she leaves him one match to light it with,
and after finally finishing the book he never

00:28:50.179 --> 00:28:53.210
wanted to write in the first place, he puts
his plan into action.

00:28:53.210 --> 00:28:56.149
First, he takes the real manuscript and hides
it under the bed.

00:28:56.149 --> 00:28:59.240
Then he takes the cover page and puts it over
an empty stack of papers.

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:03.130
He douses it with the lighter fluid and calls
Annie back into the room one last time.

00:29:03.130 --> 00:29:06.780
As soon as she enters, she catches the smell of the lighter fluid and freezes.

00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:10.370
But when Paul strikes the match, it doesn’t
light; not until the third attempt, and as

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:14.370
soon as Annie realizes what he’s trying
to do, she screams, drops the expensive champagne

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:16.350
and charges him to try to stop him.

00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:20.299
As it goes up in flames, Paul tells her that it
was the best thing he ever wrote, and as she

00:29:20.299 --> 00:29:24.820
reaches down to try to save the burning pages,
he strikes her in the back with the typewriter,

00:29:24.820 --> 00:29:26.500
which weighs nearly 50 lbs.

00:29:26.500 --> 00:29:27.899
This knocks her down into the flames.

00:29:27.899 --> 00:29:30.690
He can smell her skin burning as she bellows.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:33.379
But that was where things stopped going according
to plan.

00:29:33.379 --> 00:29:37.049
She rose back up, but tripped over the typewriter,
so Paul fell on her and stuffed her mouth

00:29:37.049 --> 00:29:39.999
full of ashes, and she’s unable to stop
him for some time.

00:29:39.999 --> 00:29:43.740
When she finally bucks him off, she ends up
tripping on the typewriter for a second time,

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:46.620
and this time her head strikes the mantelpiece
and knocks her out.

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:51.169
But like a nightmare, Annie Wilkes just won’t
die, and he ends up having to pry himself

00:29:51.169 --> 00:29:55.309
away from her, something he’s only able
to do because the leg she grabbed had no foot

00:29:55.309 --> 00:29:56.409
for her to hold onto.

00:29:56.409 --> 00:29:58.629
He crawls to the bathroom and locks himself
in there.

00:29:58.629 --> 00:30:00.409
He takes three capsules of Novril.

00:30:00.409 --> 00:30:02.879
And that -- is what we call a relapse.

00:30:02.879 --> 00:30:06.320
Eventually he wakes up and realizes he can’t
hide in the bathroom forever.

00:30:06.320 --> 00:30:10.450
He’s unsure if she’s still alive, but
when he opens the bathroom door, Annie and

00:30:10.450 --> 00:30:12.440
her axe -- are waiting for him.

00:30:21.559 --> 00:30:25.980
The National Institute of Mental Health defines
Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder as a disorder

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:30.320
that develops in some people who have experienced
a shocking, scary, or dangerous event.

00:30:30.320 --> 00:30:33.919
In my opinion, the months that Paul spent
living in the house of Annie Wilkes contained

00:30:33.919 --> 00:30:35.470
all three of these prerequisites.

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:40.000
For these reasons, PTSD was hitting Paul hard
as he tried to escape the house.

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:43.400
After first imagining Annie waiting for him
outside of the bathroom, his mind interpreted

00:30:43.409 --> 00:30:47.139
her standing in every shadow as he crawled
his way back to the parlor.

00:30:47.139 --> 00:30:50.770
When he got there, the Colorado state police
had returned and he called out to them for

00:30:50.770 --> 00:30:51.770
help.

00:30:51.770 --> 00:30:54.559
Upon discovering him, they think he looks
like a nightmare come to life.

00:30:54.559 --> 00:30:57.899
Remember, at this point they thought Paul
was most likely dead, they were mostly here

00:30:57.899 --> 00:31:00.150
to find the missing officer Dwayne Kushner.

00:31:00.150 --> 00:31:04.559
When the bigger of the two cops, Wicks recognizes
Paul, he can only remember him as “the missing

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:05.060
writer”.

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:06.360
“Christ, can’t you see?”

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:07.060
Wicks asked.

00:31:07.060 --> 00:31:08.700
“It’s the guy Kushner was looking for.

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:09.360
The writer.

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:11.320
I can’t remember his name, but it’s him.”

00:31:11.320 --> 00:31:13.920
“Thank God,” the scrawny man said.

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:14.660
“What?”

00:31:14.660 --> 00:31:15.760
Wicks bent toward him, frowning.

00:31:15.780 --> 00:31:18.360
“Thank God you can’t remember my name.”

00:31:18.369 --> 00:31:21.929
The cops are confused by his reaction, but
the reason that Paul is relieved, is that

00:31:21.929 --> 00:31:24.779
his fame is what got him into this mess in
the first place.

00:31:24.779 --> 00:31:28.059
Annie claimed to be his number one fan, and
that influenced a lot of her actions.

00:31:28.059 --> 00:31:31.610
So he was glad that his rescuers were not
fans, because he just wanted to be treated

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:32.840
like a normal human being.

00:31:32.840 --> 00:31:36.580
We next see Paul nine months later -- somewhere
around April 1988.

00:31:36.580 --> 00:31:40.020
He’s got a new apartment in Manhattan, and
he still has to make frequent visits to the

00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:40.700
hospital.

00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:43.380
He could walk again, but he would have a limp
for the rest of his life.

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:47.299
Unfortunately, after his Novril relapse in
Annie Wilkes’s bathroom, he had gone into

00:31:47.299 --> 00:31:50.710
a bit of a spiral with lots of drinking and
not a lot of writing.

00:31:50.710 --> 00:31:53.100
He still misses getting his fix of Novril.

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:56.710
On the bright side, his publisher fast-tracked
his novel to print due to the massive wave

00:31:56.710 --> 00:32:00.029
of media attention garnered from his story
becoming a national headline.

00:32:00.029 --> 00:32:04.549
An unprecedented 1 million first copies were
made, so this would easily be his biggest

00:32:04.549 --> 00:32:05.200
success.

00:32:05.200 --> 00:32:08.920
He has lunch with his editor, Charlie Merill,
who begs him for a non-fiction account of

00:32:08.929 --> 00:32:12.149
his story, and estimates they could make at
least 10 million dollars on it.

00:32:12.149 --> 00:32:15.330
Paul tries to imagine himself doing that,
and thinks he would probably start it off

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:19.140
as non-fiction, but then slowly start to fudge
stuff, because he’s a fiction writer at

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:19.640
heart.

00:32:19.640 --> 00:32:23.460
After lunch, he walks home, because his doctor
wants him to practice walking for a mile each

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:23.960
day.

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:28.040
So after everything, he really ended up back
where he started, an addict relying on alcohol

00:32:28.049 --> 00:32:30.960
to get him through the day, and not living
up to his full potential.

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:34.980
On the way back, he sees a kid pushing a caged
skunk in a shopping cart, which is a strange

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:36.549
sight, even for New York City.

00:32:36.549 --> 00:32:37.740
This gives him an idea...

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:41.090
When he gets home he takes an aspirin instead
of drinking, and for the first time in over

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:45.309
a year, seriously gets to work on a new novel
about a New Yorker who discovers a skunk in

00:32:45.309 --> 00:32:46.309
his apartment.

00:32:46.309 --> 00:32:48.779
There is a poetic symmetry to the way he starts
this new novel.

00:32:48.779 --> 00:32:52.980
When he started Fast Cars, the treasured one
that Annie made him burn, it was also because

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:56.940
of something he had while walking in New York,
so I think the meaning of this ending is that

00:32:56.940 --> 00:33:00.950
the entire traumatic experience taught him
that he is better off without the drugs, and

00:33:00.950 --> 00:33:04.769
this is him moving on and getting back to
his healthy addiction, creating novels.

00:33:04.769 --> 00:33:08.440
Paul is one of the most personal characters
that King has ever written, it’s definitely not

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:13.070
easy to create a gateway character to represent
himself and be as honest as he was about all

00:33:13.070 --> 00:33:14.070
of his flaws.

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:18.629
We would, however, see a new take on the character
portrayed by actor James Caan in the film

00:33:18.629 --> 00:33:20.140
adaptation of Misery.

00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:23.889
That is… the film adaptation of the Stephen
King novel, not the film adaptation of the

00:33:23.889 --> 00:33:24.889
Paul Sheldon novel…

00:33:24.889 --> 00:33:29.059
So join me in the next video as I break down
everything you might have missed in Misery.

00:33:29.059 --> 00:33:33.690
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring that deathbell for

00:33:33.690 --> 00:33:34.690
notifications.

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:36.780
I’ll see you in the one.

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:39.540
Assuming we stay inside.

